<!--
  The entry points for Qodana's dead-code inspection (`UnusedDeclaration`), kept HERE - shared config, in
  the code-quality-config submodule, beside the inspection profile at ../profiles/java.yaml - rather than in
  `.idea/misc.xml`, so the gate depends on no gitignored IDE directory and on nobody's local setup.
  `qodana_prepare` in the consuming project's .github/scripts/lint_and_tests.sh copies this directory into
  that project's scan working dir (.qodana/idea-config) and mounts THAT over
  `/data/project/.idea` inside the scan container, so the developer's own `.idea/` is invisible to the scan
  and is never read or written. A consuming project therefore needs the submodule checked out before the
  scan will run (`git submodule update` with the init flag; the flag cannot be spelled here, as a double
  hyphen is illegal inside an XML comment and makes this whole file unparseable).

  The obvious alternatives both failed. A read-only bind mount of this file over `.idea/misc.xml` fails the
  run outright with "This project contains no modules", because the scan WRITES that path when it sets the
  JDK and imports the Maven module. Swapping the file in for the duration of a run and restoring it
  afterwards works, but for those minutes an open IDE reads a config with no ProjectRootManager in it,
  drops the project JDK, and writes back a stripped file - so it quietly wrecks the developer's setup.

  WHY IT IS NEEDED. `jetbrains/qodana-jvm-community` is built on IDEA Community, which has no CDI, JAX-RS or
  Jakarta Persistence plugins - so nothing the framework instantiates is recognised as reachable, and every
  resource, bean, entity and injected field is reported as unused. Measured on this project: 238 `unused`
  findings with the four-annotation list this replaced, 0 with the list below. None of the 238 was real dead
  code. `UnusedDeclaration` is the one inspection this whole step exists for (it is the only check in the
  gate that sees an unused PUBLIC declaration), so the answer had to be to teach it the entry points rather
  than to switch it off.

  THE FILENAME IS THE CONTRACT, not a label. Every file in this directory is copied to the same relative
  path under `.idea/`, and the IDE decides which components to read from a file by that name alone -
  `EntryPointsManager` is looked for in `misc.xml` and nowhere else. Measured: renaming this file to
  `entry-points.xml` is not an error and prints no warning, it simply reports 756 problems instead of 470,
  because nothing here is read at all. Add configuration by adding `overrides/<the .idea filename>`; several
  unrelated components share one file here exactly as they do in a real `.idea`.

  IT HAS TO BE A COMPLETE ONE. `EntryPointsManager` alone fails the run with "This project contains no
  modules": there is no `.idea/modules.xml` in this project, so `MavenProjectsManager` below is the only
  thing that tells the IDE to import the module from the POM, and without it the scan has nothing to
  inspect. A run can appear to survive without it by reusing module state left in .qodana/cache from an
  earlier run, and then fail on the very next run once that state is rewritten - so do not trim this file
  down to the list. `ProjectRootManager` is deliberately NOT here: it pins a project JDK by IDE-local name
  (`openjdk-26`), which means nothing on a CI checkout - the scan configures its own SDK.

  WHAT EACH ENTRY BUYS. Marking a TYPE only makes the type itself reachable - members are NOT covered - so
  the JAX-RS verbs are listed alongside `@Path`: without them every `@GET`/`@POST` method (and every DTO
  built inside one) is still reported. Three declarations remain that no annotation can cover, and they
  carry an inline `@SuppressWarnings("unused")` naming their real caller instead: `TextOutcome.Failure
  .field()`, `SessionConfig.cleanupInterval()` and `RegisterRequest`'s Jackson-invoked constructor.

  ADDING TO THE LIST. Keep `list size` in step with the number of items and the indices contiguous from 0 -
  that is the IDE's own serialisation format. The stale-cache trap that makes an edit here look like it did
  nothing (the container restores its own cached `.idea` at the start of every run) is handled for you:
  qodana_prepare drops that cached copy before each scan.
-->
<project version="4">
  <component name="EntryPointsManager">
    <list size="19">
      <item index="0" class="java.lang.String" itemvalue="io.quarkus.qute.TemplateExtension" />
      <item index="1" class="java.lang.String" itemvalue="io.quarkus.scheduler.Scheduled" />
      <item index="2" class="java.lang.String" itemvalue="io.quarkus.test.junit.QuarkusTest" />
      <item index="3" class="java.lang.String" itemvalue="io.smallrye.config.ConfigMapping" />
      <item index="4" class="java.lang.String" itemvalue="jakarta.enterprise.context.ApplicationScoped" />
      <item index="5" class="java.lang.String" itemvalue="jakarta.inject.Inject" />
      <item index="6" class="java.lang.String" itemvalue="jakarta.interceptor.Interceptor" />
      <item index="7" class="java.lang.String" itemvalue="jakarta.persistence.Column" />
      <item index="8" class="java.lang.String" itemvalue="jakarta.persistence.Entity" />
      <item index="9" class="java.lang.String" itemvalue="jakarta.persistence.Id" />
      <item index="10" class="java.lang.String" itemvalue="jakarta.persistence.PreUpdate" />
      <item index="11" class="java.lang.String" itemvalue="jakarta.ws.rs.DELETE" />
      <item index="12" class="java.lang.String" itemvalue="jakarta.ws.rs.GET" />
      <item index="13" class="java.lang.String" itemvalue="jakarta.ws.rs.PATCH" />
      <item index="14" class="java.lang.String" itemvalue="jakarta.ws.rs.POST" />
      <item index="15" class="java.lang.String" itemvalue="jakarta.ws.rs.PUT" />
      <item index="16" class="java.lang.String" itemvalue="jakarta.ws.rs.Path" />
      <item index="17" class="java.lang.String" itemvalue="jakarta.ws.rs.ext.Provider" />
      <item index="18" class="java.lang.String" itemvalue="org.jboss.resteasy.reactive.server.ServerExceptionMapper" />
    </list>
  </component>
  <!--
    THE MODULE MODEL DOES NOT LIVE HERE, and cannot be made to. A Maven project is imported by IntelliJ's
    external-system integration, which writes the modules into the IDE system directory - for this gate,
    .qodana/cache/idea/*/projects/*/external_build_system/ - no matter what this file says. Two attempts
    to change that both failed and are recorded so they are not tried a third time: omitting
    ExternalStorageConfigurationManager (it defaults to ON, so this achieved nothing) and then setting it
    explicitly to enabled="false" (external-system storage is a different mechanism, so also nothing).

    The consequence is the one that matters: `.idea` is rebuilt from this file on every run, while the
    module model persists in the CACHE. When the two disagree the scan dies with "This project contains no
    modules. Inspection cannot proceed." - and because the bad state is in the cache rather than the code,
    it survives a git pull, reappears on whichever machine happened to run an older config, and looks
    intermittent. qodana_prepare therefore drops the cached model before EVERY run - it re-imports in
    seconds against a warm index, so keeping it saved nothing and only created a way to go stale.
  -->
  <component name="MavenProjectsManager">
    <option name="originalFiles">
      <list>
        <option value="$PROJECT_DIR$/pom.xml" />
      </list>
    </option>
  </component>
</project>
